{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And he buried him.</b> — He buried himself. And so you find in the divine chariot of Ezekiel (Ezekiel 10:22): ״מראיהם ואותם״ — their appearance and themselves, that is, and their very selves. And the matter of his entering into the cave in the valley. Rabbi Yishmael would expound these three instances of the accusative את: ״ויקבר אותו״ — and he buried him; likewise (Numbers 6:13) ״יביא אותו אל פתח אהל מועד״ — he shall bring him to the entrance of the tent of meeting; and likewise (Leviticus 22:16) ״והשיאו אותם עון אשמה״ — and they shall bear them the iniquity of guilt." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״ויקבר אותו בגי״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, buried him. בג״י in gematria is י״ה. And this was not said of Moses alone but of all the righteous, as it is said (Isaiah 58:8): ״והלך לפניך צדקך כבור ה׳ יאספך״. And further, you find the word ויקבר placed adjacent to the distinctive Name, and we expound what is adjacent. And the matter of the burial is that the Holy One, blessed be He, buried him: the earth opened for him by way of a miracle, and he entered there and died, and afterward it closed up." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say further that since burial is a matter of concealment and hiding, the verse may be interpreted with reference to the soul — and it says ״ויקבר אותו בגי״, that He concealed and hid him within the thirteen canopies in which the righteous take delight in the Garden of Eden, as they said of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai that he sat on thirteen canopies of beaten gold. And the word מוא״ב totals מ״ט, and ״באר בארץ מואב״ — in the land of the living — together with מ״״ט, and if so, the missing alef in the word בגי teaches us these two things, which are the elevation of Moses in body and in soul. And likewise the Torah revealed to us the days of the world through the missing bet in the word ששת, and the same judgment applies to every missing or extra letter in the Torah: even though the matter is not manifest to us, each and every one contains a wondrous reason. Understand this." "<b>Opposite Beit Peor.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded that the accusers who confronted Moses at Sinai — of whom he was afraid — encountered him, as I wrote in the section of Korah (Numbers 17:11) on the verse ״יצא הקצף״. And because Moses had to stand against the fifth one, called חמה, who opens his mouth wide against Israel, therefore Moses in his lifetime wanted to overcome him and dug from him a name, in order to weaken his power so that he would not open his mouth against Israel — in the manner of what the men of the Great Assembly did with the evil inclination, that they blinded it and covered it. And also in his death he wanted to bury himself there, so that the accuser would see Moses' grave and not be permitted to rise and accuse." "<b>And no man knows of his burial.</b> Its sense is: the place of his burial — and Scripture lacks the word 'place' because no place was known for it."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:46.077833778Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}